# Recorded device output is data, not source. Store it byte for byte.
#
# NMEA terminates a sentence with CRLF, and these captures carry it -- some with CRLF throughout,
# some with a lone CR, some with both in one file. With core.autocrlf=input, which is set on at
# least one machine here, git rewrites those to LF on commit. That silently edits third-party test
# data and throws away a property the parser is supposed to handle: SentenceRegistry.parse trims
# CR and LF, and the corpus is where that gets exercised against real hardware output.
marine-api/src/jvmTest/resources/data/** -text

# The browser demos' feeds, for the same reason: each is a slice of one of those captures and is
# CRLF-terminated like the hardware that produced it. Normalized to LF they would still parse -- the
# reader handles all three terminators -- but they would stop being truthful samples. Matched by
# glob rather than named one by one, so a log added for a new demo is covered without anyone having
# to remember this file.
examples/src/jsMain/resources/*.log -text
